810
01:07:54,677 --> 01:07:59,052
So this is something that again we started with Antonio Frankie long time ago now in 2018.

811
01:07:59,102 --> 01:08:04,711
So we said let's say we have a model of a robot whatever it is we just have to know it.

812
01:08:04,761 --> 01:08:06,745
Then we have some tracking task.

813
01:08:06,795 --> 01:08:11,011
We want to track a trajectory and we have some controller whatever it is.

814
01:08:11,061 --> 01:08:11,430
Yeah.

815
01:08:11,460 --> 01:08:17,149
Now the point here is that the model of the robot has some parameters P that we don't know and the

816
01:08:17,199 --> 01:08:24,346
controller uses some nominal value of these parameters and there is a mismatch between the real parameters and the nominal ones.

817
01:08:24,396 --> 01:08:26,868
So we want to optimize the trajectory.

818
01:08:26,918 --> 01:08:28,001
This was the point.

819
01:08:28,051 --> 01:08:30,348
We don't want to build a fancy controller.

820
01:08:30,398 --> 01:08:36,227
We take whatever we have but we want to optimize the motion the trajectory such that the closed loop

821
01:08:36,277 --> 01:08:42,450
so the the the the execution of this task will be insensitive to the discrepancies between P and PC.

822
01:08:42,500 --> 01:08:44,463
So how to do this?

823
01:08:44,513 --> 01:08:52,590
We came up with this metric this sensitivity matrix which basically is the variation of the evolution of the state in closed loop.

824
01:08:52,640 --> 01:08:59,433
So for the robots and the controller against variations on the parameters evaluated at the nominal values.

825
01:08:59,483 --> 01:09:06,430
So this is a matrix that you can compute easily and it can be used as a metric a metric for optimization for generating motion plans.

826
01:09:06,480 --> 01:09:06,910
Okay.

827
01:09:06,960 --> 01:09:10,139
So it can be used as a robustness metric.

828
01:09:10,189 --> 01:09:15,822
Now this matrix doesn't have a close form but you can come up with a very easily with an OD

829
01:09:15,872 --> 01:09:22,840
a standard OD that you for propagate and again this the point here is that this step is completely systematic.

830
01:09:22,890 --> 01:09:29,481
This can always be done as long as you can compute the Jacobians of your robot dynamics and control.

831
01:09:29,531 --> 01:09:34,190
So the assumption now is that uh these jacobians are computable.

832
01:09:34,240 --> 01:09:37,400
So the robot is differentiable the control is differentiable.

833
01:09:37,450 --> 01:09:41,884
But if you can do that this can be computed you for integrated and you get your matrix.

834
01:09:41,934 --> 01:09:44,542
Okay there is nothing to invent at this stage.

835
01:09:44,592 --> 01:09:47,840
So now once you have this matrix you can set up an optimization problem.

836
01:09:47,890 --> 01:09:51,664
So for example these are you know the first ideas that we had at the time.

837
01:09:51,714 --> 01:09:57,323
Let's optimize the trajectory that we want to track such that you know we minimize a metric of this

838
01:09:57,373 --> 01:10:03,844
matrix at the final time or we minimize the integral of the norm or whatever you want to optimize.

839
01:10:03,894 --> 01:10:04,294
Okay.

